import StateGuide from '../../../layouts/StateGuide.astro';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Is every New York residential lien due in four months?', answer: 'No. Lien Law section 10 applies a four-month period to qualifying single-family dwelling improvements and eight months to other covered private improvements. The statute includes a developer-owned subdivision exception, so a residential label alone does not decide the deadline.' },
  { question: 'What happens if I serve the owner but do not file proof?', answer: 'Section 11 requires proof of owner service to be filed with the county clerk within 35 days after the notice of lien is filed. Failure to file that proof terminates the notice as a lien.' },
  { question: 'Can a New York mechanics lien be extended automatically?', answer: 'Section 17 generally provides a one-year lien duration. Some liens can be extended by filing an extension, but single-family dwelling liens require a court order. Enforcement and extension procedures should be reviewed before the first year expires.' },
];
const sources = [9, 10, 11, '11-B', 17].map(s => ({ title: `New York Lien Law § ${s}`, url: `https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/LIE/${s}` }));
---
<StateGuide name="New York" slug="new-york" title="New York Mechanics Lien: 4/8-Month Filing & Service" description="Understand New York's four- or eight-month lien filing window, verified notice contents, owner service, 35-day proof deadline, and one-year duration." heading="New York mechanics lien: choose the filing window and complete service" summary="A New York private-improvement lien is generally filed within eight months after the last qualifying work or material, or four months for qualifying single-family dwelling improvements. Owner service and filing proof of service are separate tasks that follow their own deadlines." faqs={faqs} sources={sources}>
  <nav aria-label="On this page"><a href="#property">Property classification</a> · <a href="#notice">Notice contents</a> · <a href="#service">Service and proof</a> · <a href="#duration">Lien duration</a> · <a href="#faq">Questions</a></nav>
  <h2 id="property">Classify the property before counting calendar months</h2>
  <p>New York Lien Law section 10 allows filing during work or within eight months after the contract's completion, final work, or final material furnishing, measured from the last item. Qualifying single-family dwelling improvements use four months. The statute excludes certain developer-owned subdivision dwellings from that shorter classification. Retainage has its own 90-day provision measured from when it was due to be released.</p>
  <p>Verify the actual property and ownership facts. A condominium, an apartment building, and a developer's unsold subdivision house cannot safely be classified from the word “residential” on an invoice. Record what you checked and ask counsel to resolve uncertain classification before relying on the longer window.</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>General private-project timing</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Notice of lien, qualifying single-family dwelling</td><td>4 calendar months after the statutory furnishing/completion event</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Notice of lien, other covered improvements</td><td>8 calendar months after that event</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Owner service</td><td>Within 5 days before or 30 days after filing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>File proof of owner service</td><td>Within 35 days after filing</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Four months is not 120 days, and eight months is not 240 days. Use the statute's calendar unit. Keep the last substantive work record available rather than using the date of a collection call as the start of the period.</p>
  <h2 id="notice">What the verified notice of lien needs</h2>
  <p>Section 9 identifies the lienor and business details, attorney if any, owner and known interest, hiring party, work or materials and value, unpaid amount, first and last furnishing, and identifiable property. It also asks whether the property involves a single-family dwelling. Verification is part of the notice; a signed invoice is not the same document.</p>
  <p>Prepare from source records. List the claimant that actually contracted, identify the owner's interest, and reconcile each unpaid item against received payments. Use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/form/">form preparation guide</a> to gather the contract, ledger, title information, and work description before drafting.</p>
  <p>File with the county clerk where the property is situated. A property spanning counties requires filings in each relevant county. Check the local clerk's indexing instructions, including block identification where applicable, and current payment and submission requirements. Preserve the filed copy with its actual timestamp.</p>
  <h2 id="service">Owner service and proof filing deserve separate calendar entries</h2>
  <p>Section 11 generally permits owner service during the five days before filing or the 30 days after it. Permitted methods and fallback procedures depend on whether the owner is an individual or corporation and the recipient's availability. Follow the statutory method rather than assuming email is sufficient.</p>
  <p>The filed proof has a 35-day deadline after the lien filing. Keep the service affidavit, mailing or personal-delivery evidence, and clerk's proof-filing receipt in the same folder. Section 11-B also requires certified-mail copies to relevant contracting parties within five days before or thirty days after filing, with proof filed within thirty-five days. Lower-tier claimants can need an additional contractor copy. Check the full recipient list; missing proof can terminate the lien.</p>
  <h3>A practical service handoff</h3>
  <p>Before submitting the lien, assign the owner-service task and the proof-filing task to named people. Give each the exact filed document, recipient information, and calendar due date. Close the tasks only after collecting delivery evidence and the clerk's confirmation, respectively.</p>
  <h2 id="duration">Do not treat the first-year expiration as a recording deadline</h2>
  <p>Section 17 generally limits the lien to one year after filing unless enforcement and a notice of pendency, or an authorized extension, preserve it. Single-family dwelling extensions require a court order. Obtain advice early enough to prepare the correct preservation step; merely updating an internal calendar does not extend the lien.</p>
  <p>A <a href="/notice-of-intent-to-lien/">notice of intent to lien</a> can explain a payment demand, but does not replace filing, owner service, proof filing, or preservation. After payment, use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/release/">recorded-lien release workflow</a> and check whether the settlement also calls for a <a href="/lien-waiver/conditional/">conditional waiver</a>.</p>
  <h2>Working across state lines?</h2>
  <p><a href="/mechanics-lien/illinois/">Illinois also uses a four-month period</a>, but for third-party protection, with separate subcontractor notices. <a href="/mechanics-lien/north-carolina/">North Carolina measures filing and enforcement from last furnishing</a> and uses a lien-agent system. Select the property state in the <a href="/mechanics-lien/">mechanics lien directory</a>; similar numbers do not make the documents interchangeable.</p>
</StateGuide>
